confirmed.evidence:
KEY_IN_TEXT now ends a match at `(?:[:=]|%3[ad])` (flags 'gi'). The last character of '%3d' or '%3a' is a word character. After such a match, the scan resumes at the next key, and `(?<![\w.-])` fails there because the key is preceded by 'd' or 'a'.

Before this round, the same text matched a key such as '3dpassword': it starts after '%', which isn't in [\w.-], and SECRET_NAME finds 'pass' in it. The reply says a percent-escape lookbehind was removed as redundant ('the key after %22 is found anyway'). It isn't redundant once an earlier key's match has eaten the separator.

probe-regress3 (field 'note'; columns are 2ec0bba / 7193877 / 4a284f9):
- `x%3dpassword=Zq9SeCr3tVal`: masked / masked ('[text with a secret-looking key, 25 characters, 027a5b88]') / CLEAR, shown as is;
- `x%3Apassword: …`: masked / masked / CLEAR;
- `value%3a<secret>--db-token=`: masked / masked / CLEAR;
- `state%3A<secret>token%22%3dtoken:`: masked / masked / CLEAR.

fuzz-mask, seed 11, 40K cases from a percent-heavy alphabet: 56 regressions against 7193877, all of this class. The shape needs a non-secret key, an encoded '=' or ':', and then a secret key with no '&', '%26' or space between, which means mixed or partial encoding. It is narrow, but both previous heads masked it, so it is a regression of masking.

The attribute run can't pass a '<', so a tag such as `<password hint="a<b">` never reaches its '>' and TAG_IN_TEXT never matches it. KEY_IN_TEXT doesn't match it either ('hint' is not secret), and neither does the closing tag, because '/' isn't a name character. HTML5 allows '<' in attribute values; XML doesn't.

