fuzz-mask, seed 1, 30K mixed cases: 1,300 regressions against 7193877, and nearly every deduped sample is this class. Without '<' inside attribute values (seed 7, 40K cases) there are 0.

The round-4 fix proposal caused this. The fixes reviewer listed 'tag `[^<>]`, my suggested fix' as an equivalent mutant, and fixcheck-tag only tried tags with no '<' in their attributes. The CHANGELOG's 'a tag's attributes stop at the next `<`' describes the change, but README's 'what masking misses' doesn't list the shape. The shape needed 7193877's attribute support, so 2ec0bba showed it too. Against the 7193877 baseline this lens compares to, it is a regression of masking.

minor.evidence:
probe-words.mjs, neutral field `note`, SECRET 'hunter2Zq'.

Shown in clear:
- `s=%7B%22pw%22%3A%22hunter2Zq%22%7D`;
- `next=%2Fx%3Fa%3D1%26pw%3Dhunter2Zq`;
- `q=%26pin%3Dhunter2Zq`;
- `npmrc=registry%3Dx%0A_auth%3Dhunter2Zq`;
- `registry=x\\n_auth=hunter2Zq`;
- `a=1\\npw=hunter2Zq`.

Masked whole, as controls:
- `pw=…` and `a=1&pw=…`;
- `%7B%22password%22%3A…`;
- `a=1\\npassword=…`.

The cause: KEY_IN_TEXT's `(?<![\w.-])` refuses a key that starts after `%22`, `%26` or `\n`. The match then starts at the escape's hex digits or at 'n', so it reads the keys '22password', '26pin' and 'npw'. SECRET_NAME finds 'pass' anywhere in a key, so '22password' still matches. But this round's word-only names (`(?:^|[-_])(?:pin|pw|psk)(?:[-_]|$)|^_?auth$`) never match.

The reply says the percent-escape lookbehind was removed as 'redundant (the key after %22 is found anyway)'. That holds only for names that match anywhere in a key.

README:414 claims 'A key counts at a word's start, quoted or not (… one percent-encoded …)'. README:403 adds 'pin, pw and psk as words of their own, and auth or _auth'. The npm `_auth` this round targets shows when its .npmrc is percent-encoded or held as an escaped string. These are not regressions (all were in clear at 7193877).

probe-mask.mjs at 4a284f9, neutral field. Each of these shows 'hunter2Zq' in clear:
- a Go DSN whose password holds '@': `root:p@sshunter2Zq@tcp(127.0.0.1:3306)/app`. DSN_USERINFO's `[^\s@/]` can't cross the '@', though the URL rule reads 'up to the last @';
- a valid go-sql-driver DSN with no protocol: `root:hunter2Zq@/app`;
- other networks: `@tcp6(…)` and Cloud SQL's `@cloudsql(…)`;
- unquoted HTML attributes: `<input type=password value=hunter2Zq>`. ATTR_NAME and ATTR_VALUE require quotes, and minified HTML often drops them;
- a value holding the other quote: `<add key="DbPassword" value="it'shunter2Zq" />`. ATTR_VALUE's `[^"'<>]*` stops at either quote, so nothing is replaced. A '>' inside the value has the same effect, since ATTR_TAG ends there.

README:417-418 claim `user:<secret>@tcp(…)` and `<input type="password" value="…">`. README:419's list of misses doesn't name these variants.

minor.fix:
Pick one of:
- widen the rules:
  - DSN: let the password reach the last '@' before the network, e.g. `([^\s/]{1,256})@(tcp6?|udp6?|unix|[a-z][\w-]{0,31})\(` (watch the cost, see the DSN nit), and accept `@/`;
  - ATTR_NAME and ATTR_VALUE: take `"…"`, `'…'` or an unquoted value, e.g. `(?:"([^"<>]*)"|'([^'<>]*)'|([^\s"'<>=`]+))`;
- or list these forms under 'what masking misses'.

Add a test for each form you fix.

